traceability.statement:
This is not unique to one Fortis laboratory.

A Delhi tertiary-care quality-improvement study examined critical-alert notification and found:

- delays in notification;
- failed notifications;
- missing information;
- process failures;
- need for escalation and protocol redesign.

The study explicitly treated timely critical-result notification as a patient-safety objective.

[Quality Tools and Strategy for Critical Alerts — PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9763056/)

Another study found **64% of identified delays** were attributable to intrinsic laboratory factors, including underused escalation protocols and delayed initial calls; **26%** of delays were associated with inability to identify/contact an appropriate provider.

[Quality Improvement in Critical Value Delivery — Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/jalm/article/6/4/985/6255453)

---

Focus only on:

- what happens when SMS fails;
- what happens when the first clinician cannot be reached;
- how the correct caregiver is identified;
- how many contact attempts occur;
- how escalation works;
- how acknowledgement is verified;
- what happens during shift handover;
- how unresolved critical results are tracked;
- how closure is documented.

Do **not** quantify ROI yet.

---

- delayed critical-result notification;
- provider identification problems;
- escalation failure;
- closed-loop acknowledgement failure;
- automation improving notification.

